//! norn_fleet — fan out over a list of open issues and put each one in front
//! of a norn-backed judge, in parallel, on the engine's own durability — and
//! refuse to report anything when the judging pipeline itself cannot be shown
//! to work.
//!
//! The dispatcher hands this workflow the whole issue list in one input: each
//! item carries the issue's id, its title, and a fully-built PROMPT. Building
//! the prompt is the dispatcher's job on purpose — the judge action is the
//! plain agent seam (one String prompt in, the canonical agent-outcome
//! record out), which is the one shape a norn worker started from the
//! installed binary can serve honestly, with no crate and no rebuild. A
//! structured judgement schema would demand a custom harness binary; that
//! trade is recorded in this folder's README.
//!
//! Before any issue is judged, the control step asks a question whose answer
//! the dispatcher already knows — quote a literal that exists at a known path
//! — and refuses the whole run if the reply is wrong. A judge that cannot
//! read the tree, or cannot follow an exact-reply instruction, produces
//! confident well-formatted verdicts that are indistinguishable from real
//! ones; the distinction is made here or not at all.
//!
//! Collection is STRICT on purpose: the verdicts pair with the issues BY
//! POSITION, and positional pairing dies the moment an element can be absent.
//! (The checker independently refuses element-optional lists in declared
//! types — its reason is mechanical, schema derivation cannot express element
//! optionality — but the pairing argument stands on its own.) A judgement
//! that still fails after the declared retries have absorbed transient
//! provider overload fails the whole run rather than coming back as a quiet
//! gap dressed up as a result.
//!
//! WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE: a verdict is one model's unverified opinion.
//! The control proves the pipeline can read the tree and follow an exact
//! instruction; it proves nothing about any judgement's correctness. Every
//! citation in a verdict must be read by hand before anything acts on it.
workflow norn_fleet
  timeout 2h
  input issues: [Issue]
  input control_prompt: String
  input control_expect: String
  outcome done:       type FleetReport, route success
  outcome unmeasured: type Refused,     route failure

/// One unit of fleet work. `prompt` is complete and self-contained: the judge
/// sees nothing but this string, so everything the judgement needs — the claim,
/// the paths, the standard to judge against — must already be inside it.
type Issue { id: String, title: String, prompt: String }

/// The fleet's answer. `verdicts` pairs with `issue_ids` by position, and
/// `issue_ids` carries the pairing so an answer is never orphaned from its
/// question. `attempted` is derived from the input list — a glance, not a
/// cross-check. `control_reply` ships the control's own evidence with the
/// answer: an assertion that the method worked is not a demonstration.
type FleetReport { attempted: Int, issue_ids: [String], verdicts: [String], control_reply: String }

/// A fleet that did not judge, said out loud rather than reported as a pass.
/// Carries what the control expected and what actually came back.
type Refused { control_expect: String, control_reply: String, reason: String }

/// The canonical agent-outcome record every dispatch of the agent seam
/// returns: the judge's terminal `text` and the canonical `stop_reason`.
type Reply { text: String, stop_reason: String }

worker fleet
  /// The judge. Served by `aion worker agent` with the norn harness: the
  /// prompt goes to the session verbatim, the reply comes back verbatim.
  /// The retry policy is the real overload defence — the tharsis postmortem
  /// recorded provider-overload bursts punching through in under a minute,
  /// so five retries on a 30s..5m backoff outlasts a multi-minute outage.
  /// `timeout 15m` is an authored contract carried in the package hash; the
  /// engine does not enforce it today (the workflow-level `timeout 2h`
  /// above is the enforced bound).
  action judge(prompt: String) -> Reply
    agent, timeout 15m, retry 5 backoff 30s..5m

/// Ask a question whose answer is already known, before asking any that
/// matter. A broken pipeline and a working one both return well-formed
/// strings; only a known answer can tell them apart.
step control
  judge(prompt: control_prompt) -> control_reply
  outcome proven: when control_reply.text == control_expect, route open
  outcome broken: otherwise,
    route unmeasured(control_expect: control_expect, control_reply: control_reply.text,
      reason: "the control judgement did not return the expected reply, so this pipeline cannot be shown to read the tree or follow instructions — no verdicts are reported")

step open
  distribute issue in issues

step judge_one
  judge(prompt: issue.prompt) -> judged
  judged.text -> verdict

step close
  collect verdict -> verdicts
  issues |> map(.id) -> issue_ids
  issues |> count -> attempted
  route done(attempted: attempted, issue_ids: issue_ids, verdicts: verdicts, control_reply: control_reply.text)
